Q: How to filter and download files from S3 in powershell A: <p>If you need the latest backup by date uploaded, you can use this one liner:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$latest = Get-S3Object $Bucket Folder/If/Any | Sort-Object LastModified | Select-Object -Last 1
</code></pre>
<p>If you have different types of backups in a single folder and you need to get the latest for each type of backup, then you can use:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$backups = Get-S3Object $Bucket &lt;Folder/If/Needed&gt;
foreach ($data in $database) {
    #For testing use the QBW filter
    #Once testing is over switch to proper filter.
    $filter = &quot;*$($server)_$($data)_FULL*&quot;
    #Set arbitrary old date for each filter cycle
    [datetime]$latestDate = '1970-01-01 00:00:00'
    foreach ($backup in $backups) {
        if ($backup.Key -like $filter -and $backups[1].LastModified -gt $latestDate) {
            $latest = $backup.Key
        }
    }
    Write-Output &quot;Latest backup of type $filter is: $latest&quot;
}
</code></pre>

Q: CLI Arguments ignore A: <h3>Proof by (platform-specific) counter-example</h3>
<p>This code (I saved it in <code>argv89.c</code>) invokes a program specified on its command line with zero arguments:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;errno.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;

int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
    char *prog = &quot;argc37&quot;;
    if (argc &gt; 2)
        fprintf(stderr, &quot;Usage: %s [program]\n&quot;, argv[0]);
    else
    {
        if (argc == 2)
            prog = argv[1];

        char *args[] = { NULL };
        execv(prog, args);
        fprintf(stderr, &quot;%s: failed to execute '%s' with zero arguments (%d: %s)\n&quot;,
                argv[0], prog, errno, strerror(errno));
    }
    return 1;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I modified your code to use <code>argv</code> (because my compilation options complain about unused arguments and I compile with <code>-Werror</code>), saving the source in <code>so-7988-5166.c</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

// When calling `just`, pass a different amount of arguments: `just run 6 arg1 arg2 ...;`
int main(int argc, char* argv[])
{
    // This is impossible because there is always a command name
    if (argc &lt;= 0) {
        printf(&quot;Impossible ! No arguments have been passed!\n&quot;);
    }

    if (argc == 1) {
        printf(&quot;One argument has been passed (argv[0] = [%s])\n&quot;, argv[0]);
    }

    if (argc == 2) {
        printf(&quot;Two arguments have been passed (argv[0] = [%s], argv[1] = [%s])\n&quot;, argv[0], argv[1]);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Compiling on a MacBook Pro (16-inch, 2023 with an Apple M2 Pro chip) running macOS Tahoe 26.2 and compiling with GCC 15.2.0 (<code>gcc-15 (Homebrew GCC 15.2.0) 15.2.0</code>), I get:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>$ gcc -o argv89 -O3 -g -std=c18 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -Wmissing-prototypes -Wstrict-prototypes -fno-common argv89.c
$ gcc -o so-7988-5166 -O3 -g -std=c18 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -Wmissing-prototypes -Wstrict-prototypes -fno-common so-7988-5166.c
$ ./argv89 so-7988-5166
Impossible ! No arguments have been passed!
$
</code></pre>
<p>However, when I test the same code on RHEL 8.8 (Ootpa) using GCC 15.2.0, I get a different result:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>$ gcc -o argv89 -O3 -g -std=c18 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -Wmissing-prototypes -Wstrict-prototypes -fno-common argv89.c
$ gcc -o so-7988-5166 -O3 -g -std=c18 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -Wmissing-prototypes -Wstrict-prototypes -fno-common so-7988-5166.c
$ ./argv89 so-7988-5166
One argument has been passed (argv[0] = [])
$
</code></pre>
<p>There's a fair chance that BSD-based systems would behave the same as macOS.</p>
<p>When I ran on a Solaris 11.4 system with GCC 7.5.0 (and using <code>-std=c11</code>), I get the same result as on macOS.</p>
<p>When I ran on an AIX 7..2 machine using <code>xlclang</code> version 16.01 (<code>IBM XL C/C++ for AIX, V16.1.0  (5725-C72, 5765-J12)</code>), I get the same result as on AIX.</p>
<p>So, the result is platform dependent.</p>
<ul>
<li>If you stick to Linux, you always get at least one argument, even if it is an empty string.</li>
<li>If you run on macOS, you can get zero arguments passed.</li>
<li>If you run on Solaris, you can get zero arguments passed.</li>
<li>If you run on AIX, you can get zero arguments passed.</li>
<li>If you run on BSD, you probably get the same result as with macOS.</li>
</ul>
<p>It's likely that any systems based on AT&amp;T Unix will behave as macOS does.  Linux is likely the odd man out.</p>

Q: Avalonia DataGrid - Horizontal scrolling cuts off cells when RowHeight is less than 20 A: <p><strong>TL;DR</strong> If you simply want your table rows to be <code>18px</code> tall without any glitches, then do this:</p>
<ul>
<li>Setting <code>&lt;DataGrid RowHeight=&quot;18&quot;</code> will only set the <code>DataGrid</code>'s default <code>DataGridRow.DesiredSize</code> to <code>18</code>, but you also need to set other values too.</li>
<li>You also need to override Avalonia's <code>Fluent.xaml</code>' <code>ControlTheme</code> for <code>DataGridCell</code> (and <code>DataGridColumnHeader</code> too, for consistency); specifically, set:
<ul>
<li>Change <code>MinHeight</code> from the default of <code>32</code> (I use 16, to account for 1px borders along top and bottom edges).</li>
<li>Lower <code>FontSize</code> below the default of 15.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>...as both of these properties will keep your <code>DataGridCell</code> sizes above 18px in height.</li>
</ul>
<p>Like so:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;DataGrid
    RowHeight=&quot;18&quot;
    ItemsSource=&quot;{Binding People}&quot;
    AutoGenerateColumns=&quot;True&quot;
    IsReadOnly=&quot;True&quot;
    GridLinesVisibility=&quot;All&quot;
&gt;
    &lt;DataGrid.Resources&gt;
        &lt;ControlTheme x:Key=&quot;{x:Type DataGridCell}&quot; TargetType=&quot;DataGridCell&quot;&gt;
            &lt;Setter Property=&quot;MinHeight&quot; Value=&quot;16&quot; /&gt;
            &lt;Setter Property=&quot;FontSize&quot;  Value=&quot;10&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/ControlTheme&gt;
        &lt;ControlTheme x:Key=&quot;{x:Type DataGridColumnHeader}&quot; TargetType=&quot;DataGridColumnHeader&quot;&gt;
            &lt;Setter Property=&quot;MinHeight&quot; Value=&quot;16&quot; /&gt;
            &lt;Setter Property=&quot;FontSize&quot;  Value=&quot;10&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/ControlTheme&gt;
    &lt;/DataGrid.Resources&gt;

&lt;/DataGrid&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>...gives me this, with no glitches when scrolling horizontally:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/6HA1ZEBM.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/6HA1ZEBM.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<hr />
<p>Now for the details:</p>
<h3>What's so wrong with <code>&lt;DataGrid RowHeight=&quot;&quot;&gt;</code>?</h3>
<ol>
<li>The <code>StyledProperty</code> definition of <code>RowHeight=&quot;&quot;</code> in <code>&lt;DataGrid&gt;</code> <a href="https://github.com/AvaloniaUI/Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid/blob/8e8a335b24e9b4adf5fa5da5bae2fa6107888090/src/Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid/DataGrid.cs#L502" rel="nofollow noreferrer">is defined here</a>.</li>
<li>Contrary to intuition, the <code>RowHeight=&quot;&quot;</code> value is used by <code>DatagridCellsPresenter</code> <a href="https://github.com/AvaloniaUI/Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid/blob/8e8a335b24e9b4adf5fa5da5bae2fa6107888090/src/Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid/Primitives/DataGridCellsPresenter.cs#L232" rel="nofollow noreferrer">as the basis for the <em>desired row-height</em></a> - and is <strong>not</strong> taken as a hard limit on row height, which means that any contained (descendant) content exceeding the row-height could cause the actual rendered row-height to exceed the specified <code>RowHeight=&quot;&quot;</code> value.</li>
</ol>
<p>...and in this case, as you're using the <code>Fluent</code> theme/style, be aware that the Fluent style sets its own <code>MinHeight</code> on all <code>DataGridCell</code> instances by default: <code>&lt;Setter Property=&quot;MinHeight&quot; Value=&quot;32&quot; /&gt;</code> (in <code>&lt;ControlTheme x:Key=&quot;{x:Type DataGridCell}&quot; TargetType=&quot;DataGridCell&quot;&gt;</code>).</p>
<p>For reasons I don't fully understand, Avalonia's layout system allows the children (the <code>DataGridCell</code> with <code>MinHeight=&quot;32&quot;</code>) to exceed the <code>Inf x 18</code> constraint set upon them via <code>DataGridCellsPresenter.DesiredHeight</code>.</p>
<p>...so this explains why (without any scrolling yet) the <code>RowHeight=&quot;18&quot;</code> is ignored and the rows are rendered with an actual height of 32px.</p>
<h3>Why is it that only the cells in the first column get clipped, and only when the DataGrid has a positive horizontal scroll position?</h3>
<p>It's caused by behaviour in Avalonia's DataGrid's &quot;frozen column&quot; functionality:</p>
<p>y'see, <code>DataGrid</code> has support for a frozen-first-column, just like in Excel and other DataGrids: so that any row-headers in Column 0 will remain on-screen while the user scrolls to the right - just like how column headers (i.e. Row 0) remain &quot;frozen&quot; on-screen while you scroll down a long, tall table.</p>
<p>Now, supposing Column 0 is frozen to its leftmost position in the visible <code>DataGrid</code> <em>and</em> the user has now started scrolling to the right: that means that the cells in Column 1 will be partially occluded by Column 0 (because Column 1 <em>slips behind</em> Column 0 as the user scrolls, followed by Column 2 slipping behind Column 0, then Column 3, and so on).</p>
<p>...in order to render the current partially-occluded column the <code>DataGridCellPresenter</code> (DGCP) sets a <code>Clip</code> region on all cells in that column. Then, as the column moves off-screen as the user scrolls the DGCP then removes the <code>Clip</code> from the now-offscreen column and applies it to the next column.</p>
<p>Note that multiple columns can be on-screen <em>and</em> occluded by the frozen column (e.g. if the frozen Column 0 is 200px wide, while Columns 1, 2, 3 and 4 are each 50px wide): supposing the horizontal scroll-position is now 190px then the first 3 columns will be fully occluded and will have a <code>Clip</code> mask covering their whole dimensions, while Column 4 will have a 10px slither still visible, while Columns 5 and beyond will not be clipped.</p>
<p>...this case (of not clipping subsequent columns) is handled by <code>DataGridCellPresenter.EnsureCellClip</code>: if the current cell's column's left-edge is beyond the <code>frozenLeftEdge</code> then it sets <code>cell.Clip = null</code>, thus removing all clips.</p>
<hr />
<p>Specifically, ikn <code>DataGridCellsPresenter.EnsureCellClip</code>, when called with (for example) these args: <code>( cell, ..., frozenLeftEdge: 0, cellLeftEdge: 67 )</code> then the <code>cell.Clip</code> will be set only when <a href="https://github.com/AvaloniaUI/Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid/blob/8e8a335b24e9b4adf5fa5da5bae2fa6107888090/src/Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid/Primitives/DataGridCellsPresenter.cs#L136" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>frozenLeftEdge &gt; cellLeftEdge</code></a></p>
<ul>
<li>...but, for-all columns to the right of the partially-occluded leftmost column, <code>( frozenLeftEdge &gt; cellLeftEdge ) == false</code>, so <code>cell.Clip = null</code>.</li>
</ul>
<h4>...but my original post has nothing to do with frozen columns! How is this relevant?</h4>
<p>I think this might be a bug in Avalonia - at the same time <strong>it might not be</strong>: y'see, conceptually it-can-be-said that there's no difference between these 3 cases:</p>
<ol>
<li>When Column 0 is Frozen and is 200px wide, and Column 1 is sliding underneath it as the user scrolls, partially occluding it.</li>
<li>When Column 0 is Frozen and is 0px wide, and Column 1 is sliding underneath it as the user scrolls, partially occluding it.</li>
<li>When Column 0 is not frozen; but the user is still scrolling to the right so much that Column 0 is now entirely off-screen and Column 1 is now partially off-screen.</li>
</ol>
<p>...and in the 3rd case, the DGCP is still applying a <code>Clip</code> to those cells in Column 1; though I feel it's unnecessary, but might simplify the code because it doesn't need to check for that condition specifically? I'unno.</p>
<hr />
<p>So to re-cap so far:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Under all conditions</strong> (i.e. when the DataGrid's horizontal-scroll-position is <code>0</code> or <code>&gt;= 1</code>; or whether or not any columns are frozen), the <code>DataGridCellPresenter</code> will check if the leftmost cell in its row is partially-occluded by either a frozen-column or because the leftmost cell's column is partially scrolled off-screen.
<ol>
<li>If the cell is occluded, then it applies a <code>Clip</code> to the cell based on the <code>DesiredRowHeight</code> and <strong>not</strong> the cell's actual height if it so-happens to exceed the <code>DesiredRowHeight</code>.</li>
<li>If the cell is <em>not</em> occluded then DGCP will not set a <code>Clip</code> <em>and</em> will clear any currently set clip.</li>
</ol>
</li>
</ol>
<p>This explains why no cells are clipped until you start scrolling, and why it only affects the leftmost column's cells.</p>
<hr />
<p>So in my opinion, the bugs (so-far) are:</p>
<ul>
<li>DGCP should use the <code>DataGridCell</code>'s actual layout-height for its <code>Clip</code> rectangle's height and <em>not</em> the <code>DesiredRowHeight</code>.</li>
<li><code>&lt;DataGrid RowHeight=&quot;&quot;</code> should not be used to try to set a maximum row-height. Instead, override the current theme/style's defined sizes <strong>but also</strong> address other theme/style-defined content sizes, such as font-size, padding, margins, and border width, because those all add-up to produce other effective minimum heights of their own.
<ul>
<li>Specifically, in <code>Fluent.xaml</code>, the <code>&lt;ControlTheme&gt;</code> for <code>DataGridCell</code> should have the following properties overridden:
<ul>
<li><code>&lt;Setter Property=&quot;FontSize&quot; Value=&quot;15&quot; /&gt;</code></li>
<li>`</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Ditto for <code>DataGridColumnHeader</code>'s <code>&lt;ControlTheme&gt;</code> properties too.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>...wait a second! But my rows <strong>are</strong> 18px tall - not 32px, and the clipped cells appear <strong>shorter</strong> than 18px - that doesn't add-up with what you just wrote!</h2>
<p>Actually, <em>it does</em> - it only looks like it doesn't....</p>
<p>Here's what I see on my computer, and those <code>DataGridRow</code> elements are indeed <del>18px</del> <strong>19px</strong> tall:</p>
<div class="s-table-container"><table class="s-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>100% Zoom</th>
<th>800% Zoom</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/IouoEBWk.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/IouoEBWk.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></td>
<td><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/1p0yYb3L.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/1p0yYb3L.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>---------------------</td>
<td>----------------------</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>(It's 19px because the 18px <code>RowHeight=&quot;&quot;</code> is combined with a <code>1px</code> tall horizontal border between each row, this is set on <code>PART_BottomGridLine</code>).</p>
<p><strong>However</strong>, even though each <code>DataGridRow</code> is 19px, each <code>DataGridCell</code> in each row is actually <strong>32px</strong>, but is vertically-centred within the 18px viewport (so there's 7px beyond the top and bottom edges of the <code>DataGridRow</code> that's hidden):</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/z1ycNwm5.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/z1ycNwm5.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>Yes, it's the return of the 32px from <code>Fluent.xaml</code>! *<em><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cphNpqKpKc4" rel="nofollow noreferrer">dun-dun-duuuun</a></em></p>
<hr />
<p>Let's summarize this new information:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>The <code>&lt;DataGrid RowHeight=&quot;18&quot;&gt;</code> sets a limit on the size of <code>DataGridRow</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The <code>Fluent.xaml</code> defines a <code>MinHeight=&quot;32&quot;</code> on <code>DataGridCell</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>When a <code>DataGridCell</code> element is taller than its <code>DataGridRow</code> then it gets centred and (effectively) clipped by the <code>DataGridRow</code>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Note that this &quot;clipping&quot; of <code>DataGridCell</code> is <em>not</em> using <code>DataGridCell</code>'s <code>.Clip</code> property; instead it's because the <code>DataGridRow</code>' has its own <code>ClipToBounds</code> property set to <code>true</code>. When an Avalonia parent element has <code>ClipToBounds</code> it's basically the the same thing as using HTML's <code>overflow: hidden</code> on a <code>100x100px</code> fixed-size element which contains a <code>200x200px</code> fixed-size child element.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p>Separately, I want to describe how <code>.Clip</code> works, briefly: Avalonia, like any UI or visual system, has two main concerns:</p>
<ol>
<li>The &quot;layout&quot; of components, based on content-sizes (be they fixed-sizes, constraints, and more),</li>
<li>The <em>rendering</em> or &quot;painting&quot; of those components on-screen once their layout is calculated.</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>In contrast to WinForms - and (everyday) HTML+CSS - the &quot;layout-size&quot; of an element in Avalonia is not the same thing as the &quot;render size&quot; of the same element. A bright pink rectangle element might be sized at 500x1500px - but it doesn't necessarily have to be rendered like that on-screen: a 50x50px-sized square <code>.Clip</code> rectangle can be positioned at the top-left corner of the pink rectangle, which would mean only a 50x50px pink square would appear on-screen, followed by 450x1450px of emptiness until the user scrolls down to the next laid-out element.</li>
<li>i.e. using a clipping mask/region will <em>crop</em> an element;</li>
<li>In Avalonia, when a <code>.Clip</code> rectangle is defined, it's applied or positioned relative to the top-left corner of its <code>.Bounds</code> (which is usually the top-left-corner of its layout box).</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p>Now...</p>
<ol>
<li>...recall back to how <code>DataGridCellPresenter</code> will apply a Rectangular <code>.Clip</code> to any <code>DataGridCell</code> elements in the leftmost column when partially occluded.</li>
<li>...and the <code>.Clip</code> rectangle's height is the same as the <code>&lt;DataGrid RowHeight=&quot;&quot;</code> value (18px).</li>
<li>...and that <code>.Clip</code> is applied from the top-left corner of the <code>DataGridCell</code> (which is 32px tall).</li>
<li>...and that the <code>DataGridCell</code>, being 32px tall, is vertically centered in an 18px tall <code>DataGridRow</code> with <code>.ClipToBounds</code> set.</li>
<li>...which means the <code>.Clip</code> rectangle itself will be partially obscured by the <code>DataGridRow</code>'s bounds.</li>
<li>**...which explains exactly what <a href="https://github.com/AvaloniaUI/Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid/issues/205" rel="nofollow noreferrer">you originally observed</a>!</li>
</ol>
<p>It is demonstrable, by looking at the bounding-box of the <code>DataGridCell</code> of the middle <code>DataGridRow</code> below. For clarity I made the other 4 rows fully transparent, and disabled <code>DataGridRow.ClipToBounds</code> so that you can see the full (post-<code>.Clip</code>) <code>DataGridCell</code> which would otherwise be invisible:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/9QenteoK.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/9QenteoK.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>As you can see, the post-clip size of <code>DataGridCell</code> is 18px tall, from the top-left corner, such that it slices through half of the <code>TextBlock</code> of the cell, which is exactly what we expect - and what you saw.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Appendix:</h3>
<p>Here are the <code>&lt;Window.Styles&gt;</code> I used for those screenshots:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;Window.Styles&gt;
    &lt;Style Selector=&quot;Window&quot;&gt;
    &lt;/Style&gt;
    &lt;Style Selector=&quot;ScrollBar&quot;&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property=&quot;AllowAutoHide&quot; Value=&quot;false&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property=&quot;Background&quot; Value=&quot;Cyan&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/Style&gt;
    &lt;Style Selector=&quot;TextBlock&quot;&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property=&quot;Background&quot; Value=&quot;Gold&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/Style&gt;
    &lt;Style Selector=&quot;Border#RowBorder&quot;&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property=&quot;BorderBrush&quot; Value=&quot;Red&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property=&quot;BorderThickness&quot; Value=&quot;1&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/Style&gt;
    &lt;Style Selector=&quot;Border#CellBorder&quot;&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property=&quot;Background&quot; Value=&quot;Purple&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property=&quot;BorderBrush&quot; Value=&quot;HotPink&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property=&quot;BorderThickness&quot; Value=&quot;50&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/Style&gt;
    &lt;Style Selector=&quot;DataGridCell&quot;&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property=&quot;Background&quot; Value=&quot;Brown&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property=&quot;BorderBrush&quot; Value=&quot;Magenta&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property=&quot;BorderThickness&quot; Value=&quot;1&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/Style&gt;
&lt;/Window.Styles&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>I also wrapped the <code>&lt;DataGrid&gt;</code> in a <code>&lt;Border&gt;</code> and added an additional border to the grid:</p>
<pre><code>    &lt;Border BorderBrush=&quot;Green&quot; BorderThickness=&quot;5&quot;&gt;
        &lt;DataGrid
            RowHeight=&quot;18&quot;
            ItemsSource=&quot;{Binding People}&quot;
            AutoGenerateColumns=&quot;True&quot;
            IsReadOnly=&quot;True&quot;
            GridLinesVisibility=&quot;All&quot;

            BorderThickness=&quot;5&quot;
            BorderBrush=&quot;Blue&quot;
            Background=&quot;LightGray&quot;
        &gt;
            &lt;DataGrid.Resources&gt;
                &lt;ControlTheme x:Key=&quot;{x:Type DataGridCell}&quot; TargetType=&quot;DataGridCell&quot;&gt;
                    &lt;Setter Property=&quot;MinHeight&quot; Value=&quot;16&quot; /&gt;
                    &lt;Setter Property=&quot;FontSize&quot;  Value=&quot;10&quot; /&gt;
                &lt;/ControlTheme&gt;
                &lt;ControlTheme x:Key=&quot;{x:Type DataGridColumnHeader}&quot; TargetType=&quot;DataGridColumnHeader&quot;&gt;
                    &lt;Setter Property=&quot;MinHeight&quot; Value=&quot;16&quot; /&gt;
                    &lt;Setter Property=&quot;FontSize&quot;  Value=&quot;10&quot; /&gt;
                &lt;/ControlTheme&gt;
            &lt;/DataGrid.Resources&gt;

        &lt;/DataGrid&gt;
    &lt;/Border&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>...which looks like this:</p>
